  <adoption-note date="2026-08-28">
    ADOPTED INTO THIS PROJECT, NOT DEFERRED. Owner direction 2026-08-28: "if an issue is
    important, then it should be added to this project NOT deferred to others."

    SCOPE SPLIT, deliberately. The spec puts "React/PCF Jest test architecture" OUT of scope and it STAYS out: how those tests are designed and organized is not this project business. But "no workflow runs jest at all" is a CI GAP, and CI is precisely what this project owns. 707 test files across 39 packages, zero enforced, while 923 server .cs files do run. One package has been red since 2026-06-11 under a green board.

    This is a DEFECT REPORT AGAINST THIS PROJECT OWN DELIVERABLE, not new scope. The project
    cannot honestly close while something it shipped is known-broken. That is north star #1
    ("fix it right the first time") and #3 ("no constant reds").
  </adoption-note>

  <goal>Jest runs in CI across all 39 client packages and reports honestly. The failures are baselined and visible. No test-architecture remediation is attempted.</goal>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">NEW workflow file (client-tests.yml). New files are NOT covered by the tier-file freeze, and adding jest to Tier 2 now is the single most expensive available move: it would inject an unknown red into the shadow window.</step>
    <step order="2">continue-on-error on day one. The point of Phase 1 is to SEE the surface, not to gate on it.</step>
    <step order="3">Baseline all 39 packages. The per-package pass/fail table IS the artifact of this task.</step>
    <step order="4">Use `npm install --legacy-peer-deps --no-audit --no-fund`, NOT `npm ci` -- root CLAUDE.md 12: roughly 14 of 16 Vite solutions have stale lockfiles and `npm ci` fails on them.</step>
    <step order="5">STOP THERE. Fixing the failing tests, and any jest test-architecture work, is explicitly OUT of scope for this task and this project. File what the baseline finds and hand it on with the numbers.</step>
    <step order="6">Promotion into Tier 2 as a real gate happens only AFTER the shadow window closes and AFTER the baseline is green. Do not promote in this task.</step>
  </steps>

  <notes date="2026-08-28">
    COMPLETE (Phase 1 plumbing). Full write-up: notes/095-client-test-baseline.md.

    Gap verified independently and slightly worse than #851 stated: 730 tracked client test files
    (not 707) across 40 packages (not 39), and ZERO workflows run jest — confirmed by
    `grep -rln "jest" .github/workflows/`. Also: there is NO npm workspace root, so all 40
    packages install independently. That fact drove the trigger decision.

    SHIPPED: .github/workflows/client-tests.yml — discover (dynamic matrix by SCANNING for a real
    jest test script, so it cannot drift as packages are added; rejects the npm-init stub; fails
    loudly on zero discovered rather than reporting a vacuous green) -> test (per-package,
    continue-on-error, fail-fast false, max-parallel 8, install-failure recorded DISTINCTLY from
    test-failure so a stale lockfile is not misreported as failing tests) -> summarize (per-package
    table into the job summary + 90-day artifact, with an explicit incomplete-baseline warning if
    fewer legs report than were discovered).

    DELIBERATE: does NOT run on pull_request. 40 packages with no workspace root = 40 npm installs;
    hanging that off every PR would contend for runners with the very PRs the shadow window is
    accumulating, against north star #2 ("CI must not hold up high-frequency pushes"). Phase 1 is
    to SEE the surface, not gate it, and a nightly baseline sees it equally well. Triggers are
    schedule (07:00 UTC, one hour after nightly-health at 06:00 so they do not collide) +
    workflow_dispatch. Adding pull_request is Phase 2 promotion, not an oversight.

    FREEZE-SAFE: new file only; no existing workflow touched, so the tier-file freeze is not
    engaged. In no blocking filter; every step continue-on-error.

    BASELINE: workflow_dispatch requires the workflow on the DEFAULT branch, so the full
    40-package table is generated after this PR merges and then recorded in the notes. Local
    spot-check validating the exact CI command shape found Spaarke.SdapClient FAILING at import
    time (1 suite failed, 0 tests ran) — exactly the class of finding this exists to surface.

    SCOPE HELD: no jest test file was modified. Fixing the failures is explicitly NOT this task
    and NOT this project — spec keeps "React/PCF Jest test architecture" out of scope.
  </notes>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">client-tests.yml exists and runs jest across all 39 packages</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">continue-on-error is set; the workflow cannot block a PR</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Per-package baseline table published in project notes</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No jest test file is modified by this task</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Not added to the Tier 1 or Tier 2 blocking filters</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
